      "path": "internal/runner/runner.go",
      "summary": "Sets PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX for every frame step and word to the run's own pycache directory and removes it when the run or cleanup ends.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            502,
            505
          ],
          "summary": "environment forces PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX to <run dir>/pycache, after the caller's Env changes, like the VERILEX_* values.",
          "rationale": "Drift and stamps skip __pycache__, and Python loaded a planted unchecked-hash .pyc from the checkout, so a planted cache could turn a defect green. A cache prefix makes Python ignore in-tree caches. Chosen over hashing caches because Python writes them on every run, which would make words drift after each run. A fresh directory per run also avoids stale bytecode from a same-second, same-size edit. Limit: python -E or -I ignores the variable; docs/adding-verilex.md forbids both. Cost: Python code recompiles on every run.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-word-mode-drift"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            141,
            141
          ],
          "summary": "Execute removes the run's pycache directory when it returns.",
          "rationale": "Run directories are never pruned, so per-run bytecode would grow without bound. A kept instance's own processes may recreate the directory while they live; Python treats a missing prefix as a cache miss, so that only leaves files, never wrong code. proof.sh in the task data showed 0 pycache directories left after a bytecode-writing word.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-word-mode-drift"
        }
      ]
